    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **262 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional postcode validation for owners so the app satisfies the specification while staying backward-compatible.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added an optional `postcode` string property to the `OwnerFields` schema. Since `Owner` is `allOf` `OwnerFields`, this makes postcode both an accepted input and a returned output; the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto` and MapStruct mappers pick it up automatically (matching field name \u2192 auto-mapped both directions).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `postcode` field (`@Column(name = \"postcode\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added the fixed region\u2192postcode-range table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099) and a `regionForCity` mapping (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD). In `addOwner`, when a postcode is present it's validated: must be exactly 4 digits, and if the city maps to a known region the value must fall in that region's range. A city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode. Invalid \u2192 `400`. Absent postcode is untouched, so the request contract stays backward-compatible.\n\n4. **`db/{h2,hsqldb,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 Added the `postcode` column to the `owners` table in all four dialects.\n\n**Verification against the spec's acceptance tests:**\n- `coreStoresValidPostcode` \u2014 \"2000\" stored and returned \u2713\n- `coreAcceptsOwnerWithoutPostcode` \u2014 absent postcode still accepted \u2713\n- `errorRejectsMalformedPostcode` \u2014 \"12\" rejected with 400 \u2713\n\nThe range logic is validated per city region, unknown-region cities accept any 4-digit value, and postcode is only validated when present.",
